The TLS thunk for every `mrs xN, tpidr_el0` in vendor code (80861 sites in Mali-G2's libGLES_mali.so,
96.5% of them stack-guard reads) checks that the thread's bionic TLS is set up before it returns the
address. It did so through x16/x17 saved on the stack: two stores, three loads and a stack pointer
round trip on every execution. Do the check in the target register, which the MRS writes anyway:
mrs, add, add, ldr [xT, #8], cbz, then mrs, add, add, b back on initialized threads (one load, no
stack, flags untouched as CBZ sets none); only first touch saves x16, x17, x30, calls the helper and
rejoins. Same 80-byte thunk size and the same first-touch helper.

